is version, v3)]\nL9: # Title:Fast LLM Post-training via Decoupled and Fastest-of-N Speculation\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Rongxin Cheng , cite7†Kai Zhou , cite8†Xingda Wei , cite9†Siyuan Liu , cite10†Mingcong Han , cite11†Mingjing Ai , cite12†Yeju Zhou , cite13†Baoquan Zhong , cite14†Wencong Xiao , cite15†Rong Chen , cite16†Haibo Chen L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Fast LLM Post-training via Decoupled and Fastest-of-N Speculation, by Rongxin Cheng and 10 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite17†View PDF cite18†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Rollout dominates the training time in large language model (LLM) post-training, where the trained model is used to generate tokens given a batch of prompts. This work, SpecActor, achieves fast rollout with speculative decoding that deploys a fast draft path to accelerate the unparallelizable generation, while the correctness is guaranteed by fast parallel verification of the outputs with the original model.\nL17: SpecActor addresses two foundational challenges that hinder speculation efficiency: (1) a Decoupled speculation method that overcomes the computation inefficiency issue when executing speculative decoding with relative large per-worker batch size -- a common configuration in training but unfriendly to speculation, and (2) a Fastest-of-N speculation method that selects and combines different draft methods according to the rollout progress to approximate the optimal draft method even when the best one is unknown a priori.\nL18: Extensive evaluations on production traces show that SpecActor accelerates mean rollout speed by 2.0--2.4x, with up to 2.7x speedup, over common post-training baselines. The results are consistent across both dense and MoE models and across different RL algorithms. Notably, SpecActor is 1.1--2.6x faster compared to vanilla speculative rollout in different traces. The accelerated rollout achieves 1.4--2.3x faster end-to-end training time.\nL19: Subjects:  | Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite19†arXiv:2511.16193 [cs.DC]\nL21:    | (or cite20†arXiv:2511.16193v3 [cs.DC] for this version)\nL22:    | cite21†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.16193†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Rongxin Cheng [cite22†view email ]\nL26: cite5†[v1] Thu, 20 Nov 2025 10:00:03 UTC (2,465 KB)\nL27: cite23†[v2] Fri, 21 Nov 2025 07:34:03 UTC (2,465 KB)\nL28: [v3] Tue, 23 Dec 2025 09:31:34 UTC (2,047 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Fast LLM Post-training via Decoupled and Fastest-of-N Speculation, by Rongxin Cheng and 10 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite17†View PDF L37:   * cite18†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite24†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite25†view license L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.DC\nL44: \nL45: cite26†< prev |   cite27†next > L46: \nL47: cite28†new | cite29†recent | cite30†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite31†cs L52: cite32†cs.AI L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite33†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite34†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite35†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.16947] Fine-grained MoE Load Balancing with Linear Programming (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.16947)\nciteturn2578view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2577view3\",\"lineno\":6}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 21 Nov 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 15 Jan 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Fine-grained MoE Load Balancing with Linear Programming\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Chenqi Zhao , cite7†Wenfei Wu , cite8†Linhai Song , cite9†Yuchen Xu , cite10†Yitao Yuan L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Fine-grained MoE Load Balancing with Linear Programming, by Chenqi Zhao and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) has emerged as a promising approach to scale up deep learning models due to its significant reduction in computational resources. However, the dynamic nature of MoE leads to load imbalance among experts, severely impacting training efficiency. While previous research has attempted to address the load balancing challenge, existing solutions either compromise model accuracy or introduce additional system overhead.\nL17: As a result, they fail to achieve fine-grained load balancing, which is crucial to optimizing training efficiency.\nL18: > We propose a novel parallelization strategy to achieve fine-grained load balancing in MoE systems. Our system is capable of achieving optimal load balancing in every micro-batch through efficient token scheduling across GPUs. Our experimental results demonstrate that MicroMoE improves the end-to-end training throughput by up to 47.6% compared with the state-of-the-art system, and almost consistently achieves optimal load balance among GPUs.\nL19: Comments:  | 20 pages\nL20: Subjects:  | Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2511.16947 [cs.DC]\nL22:    | (or cite14†arXi