Training-Inference Mismatch\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Ziyang Zhang , cite7†Xinheng Ding , cite8†Jiayi Yuan , cite9†Rixin Liu , cite10†Huizi Mao , cite11†Jiarong Xing , cite12†Zirui Liu L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Deterministic Inference across Tensor Parallel Sizes That Eliminates Training-Inference Mismatch, by Ziyang Zhang and 6 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite13†View PDF cite14†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Deterministic inference is increasingly critical for large language model (LLM) applications such as LLM-as-a-judge evaluation, multi-agent systems, and Reinforcement Learning (RL). However, existing LLM serving frameworks exhibit non-deterministic behavior: identical inputs can yield different outputs when system configurations (e.g., tensor parallel (TP) size, batch size) vary, even under greedy decoding.\nL17: This arises from the non-associativity of floating-point arithmetic and inconsistent reduction orders across GPUs.\nL18: While prior work has addressed batch-size-related nondeterminism through batch-invariant kernels, determinism across different TP sizes remains an open problem, particularly in RL settings, where the training engine typically uses Fully Sharded Data Parallel (i.e., TP = 1) while the rollout engine relies on multi-GPU TP to maximize the inference throughput, creating a natural mismatch between the two. This precision mismatch problem may lead to suboptimal performance or even collapse for RL training.\nL19: We identify and analyze the root causes of TP-induced inconsistency and propose Tree-Based Invariant Kernels (TBIK), a set of TP-invariant matrix multiplication and reduction primitives that guarantee bit-wise identical results regardless of TP size. Our key insight is to align intra- and inter-GPU reduction orders through a unified hierarchical binary tree structure. We implement these kernels in Triton and integrate them into vLLM and FSDP.\nL20: Experiments confirm zero probability divergence and bit-wise reproducibility for deterministic inference across different TP sizes. Also, we achieve bit-wise identical results between vLLM and FSDP in RL training pipelines with different parallel strategy. Code is available at cite15†this https URL†github.com .\nL21: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite16†arXiv:2511.17826 [cs.LG]\nL23:    | (or cite17†arXiv:2511.17826v2 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL24:    | cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.17826†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Xinheng Ding [cite19†view email ]\nL28: cite5†[v1] Fri, 21 Nov 2025 22:40:00 UTC (532 KB)\nL29: [v2] Fri, 29 May 2026 02:47:21 UTC (564 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Deterministic Inference across Tensor Parallel Sizes That Eliminates Training-Inference Mismatch, by Ziyang Zhang and 6 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite13†View PDF L38:   * cite14†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite20†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite21†view license L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.LG\nL45: \nL46: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L47: \nL48: cite24†new | cite25†recent | cite26†2025-11 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite27†cs L53: cite28†cs.CL "
